  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Potential for patchy frost tonight, Tuesday, \nand Wednesday nights across the north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA strong 1030mb high pressure system located over Ontario builds \nsouth into the area tonight, with skies clearing over the \nnorth. Anticipating for low lying valleys to decouple across \nthe north, with clear skies, high RH, and generally light winds. \nOccasional breezy winds could reduce fog chances. \n\nHigh pressure remains over the region Tuesday and Wednesday \nnights with clear skies and light winds. This will provide \nfavorable conditions for radiational cooling. Patchy/areas of \nfrost are expected Tuesday and Wednesday nights across northern \nand central portions of the region. Temperatures could fall \nbelow freezing across the normally colder northern low lying and\nvalley locations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A coastal low could impact at least Downeast\nMaine late this week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA coastal low is expected to develop across the Mid-Atlantic\nregion later this week while high pressure remains centered over\nQuebec. The track of the ocean low is still uncertain with \nblocking high pressure to the northwest of our forecast area and\nridging across the Great Lakes. Uncertainty remains regarding \nthe northern edge of the precipitation shield and whether rain \nwill reach Maine. Northerly to northeasterly winds could become \ngusty over the weekend, especially along the coast, as a tight \npressure gradient forms.",
